+
+// What a composed launch that resolved no locale presents.
+//
+// Not the host's, and not nothing: absence of a resolved locale has to mean a
+// defined default, or the served locale becomes a property of the operator's
+// shell. Mirrors COMPOSED_DEFAULT_LOCALE in python/apostate/launch.py and
+// npm/src/index.ts, which is the point -- a bare launch and a launch through
+// either package must present the same locale for the same resolution.
+inline constexpr char kComposedDefaultLocale[] = "en-US";
+
+// The first tag of an Accept-Language list: `de-DE,de` -> `de-DE`. An empty or
+// blank list yields kComposedDefaultLocale.
+BASE_EXPORT std::string ComposedLocaleTag(std::string_view accept_languages);
+
+// `de-DE` -> `de_DE.UTF-8`, the form the three LC_* variables expect.
+//
+// A tag with no region stays region-free rather than acquiring an invented
+// one, and only the first `-` becomes `_`, so `zh-Hans-CN` yields
+// `zh_Hans-CN.UTF-8` -- what python/apostate/launch.py's `_posix_locale`
+// produces for the same tag. If the host has not generated the named locale,
+// setlocale refuses it and LANGUAGE -- which takes the tag as written and
+// needs nothing generated -- still decides the application locale. Either way
+// the host's own value is gone, which is the point of writing these at all.
+BASE_EXPORT std::string PosixLocaleForTag(std::string_view tag);
+
+// Pins this process's locale environment to `tag`, so that the application
+// locale is the one this launch resolved and never the operator's shell.
+//
+// Writes LANGUAGE, LC_ALL, LC_MESSAGES and LANG -- all four, because each
+// moves the application locale on its own, so leaving any one at the
+// operator's value lets the host win through a variable nobody wrote. LANGUAGE
+// takes `tag` as written; the other three take PosixLocaleForTag(tag).
+//
+// Then, on POSIX, re-runs the two setlocale calls content/app/content_main.cc
+// makes at process start, from the "C" locale a fresh process begins in.
+// Writing the variables is not sufficient on its own: content adopted the
+// operator's environment into the C library before any embedder hook ran, and
+// glib's application-locale lookup falls back to setlocale(LC_MESSAGES, ...)
+// when LANGUAGE is unset -- so on a host that has the operator's locale
+// generated, that fallback is still theirs. Re-running the sequence reproduces
+// byte-for-byte the C-library state a launcher-set environment would have
+// produced, including the case where the composed locale is not generated here
+// and setlocale leaves "C".
+//
+// Browser process only, and single-threaded: base::Environment::SetVar and
+// setlocale are both documented as unsafe in a multi-threaded process.
+// PreSandboxStartup satisfies that -- content itself writes FC_FONTATIONS
+// immediately after it, "before creating threads" -- and it is before the
+// zygote fork, so every child inherits this environment rather than needing
+// its own copy of the decision.
+BASE_EXPORT void InstallComposedLocaleEnvironment(std::string_view tag);
+
